# flow-go configuration
#
# Every value here has a working default. Copy to .env to override; real
# environment variables always win over this file.
#
# Read by config.LoadEnv at start-up — before any command parses its flags — so a
# value set here becomes the *default* for the matching flag. An explicit flag
# always wins over this file:
#
#   FLOW_RECAPTCHA=broker   ./flow-go image "…"                     -> broker
#   FLOW_RECAPTCHA=broker   ./flow-go image "…" --captcha off       -> off
#
# There is no HTTP API and no server port. flow-go is a CLI plus a WebSocket
# bridge: the extension supplies cookies and nothing else, and no generation
# request travels through a socket. HTTP_PORT is not read and setting it has no
# effect.

# ─── Auth & Project ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The Flow project to generate into. Leave empty and the engine resolves one, in
# this order: --project-id, then this, then the account bundle, then the
# account's project list (RPC UpteDb), then a Flow tab in the browser.
#
# It also *creates* a project when the account has none, because every generation
# call carries a project id and one that names nothing is refused upstream.
# `./flow-go projects` lists what the account already has.
# FLOW_PROJECT_ID=

# Which signed-in Google account (authuser index) to act as. Chrome allows ten.
#
# Only a seed: once an account has been chosen and recorded, the stored index is
# what the engine starts on, so a deliberate choice is not undone by a restart.
# ACCOUNT_INDEX=0

# How many signed-in account indices are examined when looking for one that can
# pay for a job. Each index costs a session, a profile and a balance read, and
# the scan runs on the request path — so the default covers the common case and
# the knob covers the rest. Scanning past the last signed-in account is harmless:
# an index beyond it falls back to the default account and the repeats are
# recognised and dropped.
# ACCOUNT_SCAN_LIMIT=6

# Supply a bearer token directly, for manual testing. The ordinary path mints one
# from cookies, so this is normally unset.
# FLOW_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# Set to `off` to disable the engine's own session repair, so the raw upstream
# error surfaces instead of the engine trying to re-mint the session itself.
# Accepts: off | false | 0 | no | disabled. Anything else, or unset, leaves it on.
# FLOW_SESSION_REBUILD=

# ─── reCAPTCHA ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Every generation call carries a reCAPTCHA token. Flow refuses a request whose
# token it will not accept with PUBLIC_ERROR_UNUSUAL_ACTIVITY — and that arrives
# as HTTP 200 with no media rather than as an error, so it reads as a generation
# that silently did nothing.
#
#   auto   (default) mint over the transport. No browser needed.
#   broker           ask the attached extension / Flow page to mint. The highest
#                    score, because it runs in a real signed-in page — but it
#                    needs the extension loaded and a tab that can reach
#                    /project/<id>, which is the only page that loads the client.
#   http             force the transport; never ask the browser.
#   off              send no token. Testing only; Flow rejects it.
#
# Auto-escalation: when a submission is refused with
# PUBLIC_ERROR_UNUSUAL_ACTIVITY — and only then — the engine asks a page for one
# more token and submits once more. It does that only when a bridge is connected,
# which is what keeps `auto` browser-free, and it never loops: a second refusal
# with a better token means the assessment is refusing the *client* rather than
# the token, and the refusal is then the honest answer. Any other reason is final
# — a wrong model or another account's project is not fixed by minting again.
# FLOW_RECAPTCHA=auto

# The reCAPTCHA Enterprise site key. Only needed if Google rotates it.
# FLOW_RECAPTCHA_SITE_KEY=

# ─── Upstream & Network ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Route upstream calls through one exit IP (http:// or socks5://). Flow scores IP
# consistency, so a stable residential proxy measurably improves the odds.
# FLOW_PROXY=

# The public Flow client key. Not a secret — it ships in the browser bundle.
# FLOW_API_KEY=

# The frontend build the client claims to be, sent as `bl` on every batchexecute
# call. A stale value produces no error at all: the calls simply go out claiming
# to be an older frontend than the one they are talking to, which is why the
# compiled default is re-read from the live app rather than left alone.
#
# Read it back with:
#   curl -sS -A "<a Chrome UA>" https://flow.google.com/ \
#     | grep -oE 'boq_labs-ai-sandbox-frontend_[0-9]+\.[0-9]+_p[0-9]'
#
# Live value as of 2026-09-23, and also the compiled default:
# FLOW_BUILD_LABEL=boq_labs-ai-sandbox-frontend_20260922.00_p0

# ─── WebSocket Bridge ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Where the Chrome Bridge extension dials in.
#
# There is no HTTP port. The bridge receives account bundles and the CLI
# generates; a generation request that travelled through a listening socket would
# have to be authenticated, would have to name an account, and would make the
# process holding the browser the process that spends the credits — three things
# that split exists to avoid.
# WS_PORT=9222

# ─── Rate Limits ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Applied to the six submission RPCs — image generation, text-to-video,
# first+last frame, start frame, reference images and video edit — and to nothing
# else. Read-only calls are deliberately unpaced: polling depends on them being
# immediate, a refused read costs nothing, and spacing a poll two seconds apart
# would turn a seven-minute wait into a much longer one.
#
# Both are live as of 2026-09-23. Before that they were read only by the legacy
# REST client, which no working path uses, so setting them had no effect.
#
# They exist because a refusal has no status to react to — HTTP 200 with a reason
# in the frame — so there is nothing to back off *from* after the fact. This is
# the only local defence, and it acts before the call goes out.
# MAX_CONCURRENT_REQUESTS=4    # in-flight submissions per account
# REQUEST_MIN_INTERVAL=2.0     # seconds between submissions

# ─── Generation Defaults ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Default image model. `--model` wins over this.
#   narwhal  -> NARWHAL      (Nano Banana 2 — the default)
#   lite     -> HARBOR_SEAL  (Nano Banana 2 Lite)
#   pro      -> GEM_PIX_2    (Nano Banana Pro)
# Either the friendly name or the resolved enum is accepted.
# IMAGE_MODEL=narwhal

# Video duration, in seconds: 4 | 6 | 8 | 10. The CLI takes --duration
# (default 10).
#
# A duration the cost table does not record is not refused — the engine proceeds
# and says the balance could not be checked, because the table must not become
# the limit on what can be generated.
#
# Video quality: 360p | 720p. The CLI takes --quality (default 720p).
#
# Quality is the expensive axis rather than a cosmetic one — a 4s render costs 4
# credits at 360p against 7 at 720p — and it rides on the model key as a `_360p`
# suffix. The key is the authority: naming abra_t2v_4s_360p is priced at 360p
# whatever the flag says, and an account that cannot afford the quality it asked
# for is refused rather than quietly served a cheaper render.
#
# There is no 1080p and no 4K. The upsampler that produced them is gone.

# ─── Timings ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# POLL_INTERVAL=10     # seconds between generation status polls
# POLL_TIMEOUT=420     # seconds to wait for one render before giving up
# REQUEST_TIMEOUT=90   # seconds for one upstream HTTP call
# SESSION_TTL=2400     # seconds a minted access token is trusted (Google's live ~60m)

# ─── Storage & Paths ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Kept separate on purpose, and both directories are gitignored.
#   FLOW_DATA_DIR     the SQLite database only
#   FLOW_COOKIE_DIR   cookies, page tokens and the fingerprint only
#   FLOW_OUTPUT_DIR   downloaded images and videos
#
# All of them resolve relative to the *working directory*, so run the engine from
# flow-go/flow-go — or use the `flow` wrapper at the workspace root, which changes
# into it first for exactly this reason. A run from anywhere else creates empty
# directories beside the caller and finds none of the real ones, which surfaces
# as an auth failure rather than as a wrong directory.
# FLOW_DATA_DIR=./data
# FLOW_COOKIE_DIR=./cookies
# FLOW_OUTPUT_DIR=./output
# FLOW_DB_PATH=./data/flow.db

# ─── Not documented here on purpose ──────────────────────────────────────────

# FLOW_PAYGATE_TIER and FLOW_REFERER are read by internal/flowapi, the legacy
# aisandbox REST client. No working generation path uses that surface — the app
# moved to flow.google.com and that API key is referrer-restricted to
# labs.google, which is why generation could never succeed there. Setting either
# has no effect on a generation, so they are left out rather than presented as
# knobs.
